Deliberately not an open-source licence. Farewatch was built as a side project
with the intention that it might eventually earn money, and a permissive licence
would let anyone stand up a competing service from the same code. If that stops
being the goal, MIT is a one-file change.

Third-party data used at runtime is not covered by this notice and remains
subject to its own terms:

  * Exchange rates from api.frankfurter.dev (European Central Bank reference
    rates) and open.er-api.com.
  * Deal listings fetched from the public RSS/Atom feeds named in
    config/sources.json. Those remain the property of their publishers; this
    software links to them and does not republish their content.
